Z||000000||Formula88||08-27-2001||02:40 PM||||Ok, my cat melted on me a few months ago and I recently got it replaced with a used one. (thanks, Hoop!)  Anyway, I want to make sure this one doesn't get toasted, so I'm looking for ideas on what could have caused the old converter to die.  It had been replaced about 2-3 years ago (before I bought the car).  The car seems to run fine.  I raced it against LZeitgeist's 62K mile original Formula and we're about as exactly even as you can get, so I figure that's a good benchmark that it's running right - or close to it.  It did appear to blow a bit of black smoke (gas) when under heavy throttle without the cat.  Also, my EGR tube is blocked off until I can get a replacement.  I'm wondering if either of these two items could plug up the converter.  I'm planning on getting a new O2 sensor soon as preventative maintenance, but I'm getting no codes in the ECM, and it seems to run great.<P>BTW, I'm running premium fuel since I don't have the EGR functioning.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||08-27-2001||04:05 PM||||the O2 sensor needs to be replaced every 30k miles, like its a normal maintaince thing.<P>  it gradually becomes less and less sensitive, which makes the car run richer and richer - but it wont give you an error code until its really fouled out.||||reg||10
Z||000002||btoth||08-27-2001||05:14 PM||||I was <I>told</I> that gas can ruin the insides of the converter if it gets in there, as well as coolent.||||reg||1
